Q: How to find X and Y position of text caret/cursor A: <p>There's some confusion in this thread: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/63769617/6243352">the accepted answer</a> does appear to work fairly well, but only in a <code>contenteditable</code> element. It's not exactly clear how to use it.</p>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/74884245/6243352">This alternative answer</a> is appealing in that it works in a textarea, but unfortunately, it has at least one serious bug: if you press enter a number of times in the textarea, then click on the cursor, it logs coordinates far below the cursor position. That's a deal breaker for me.</p>
<p>Here's a runnable example showing the accepted answer in action, with an adjustment to handle an edge case. When you press Enter to start a new line, the rect values all log as 0 unless a zero-width span is added into the mix:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const getCursorRect = () =&gt; {
  const selection = window.getSelection();

  if (!selection.rangeCount) {
    return null;
  }

  const range = selection.getRangeAt(0).cloneRange();
  range.setStart(selection.focusNode, selection.focusOffset);
  range.collapse(true);
  const marker = document.createElement("span");
  range.insertNode(marker);
  const rect = marker.getBoundingClientRect();
  marker.remove();
  return rect;
};

const showCursor = () =&gt; {
  const rect = getCursorRect();
  const div = document.createElement("div");
  div.style.position = "absolute";
  div.style.height = div.style.width = "5px";
  div.style.background = "red";
  div.style.left = rect.x + "px";
  div.style.top = rect.y + "px";
  document.body.appendChild(div);
  setTimeout(() =&gt; div.remove(), 1000);
  console.log(rect);
};

const div = document.querySelector("div");
div.addEventListener("keyup", showCursor);
div.addEventListener("click", showCursor);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>[contenteditable] {
  min-height: 100px;
  width: 200px;
  border: 1px solid black;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div contenteditable="true"&gt;type stuff here!&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Is it any way to highlight text in &lt;textarea&gt;? A: <p>By &quot;highlight&quot;, I assume you mean &quot;select text&quot;?</p>
<p>If so, here's a runnable example to illustrate <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79257673/6243352">this answer</a>:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const selectBar = () =&gt; {
  const ta = document.querySelector("textarea");
  ta.focus();
  ta.setSelectionRange(4, 7);
};

const selectEverything = () =&gt; {
  const ta = document.querySelector("textarea");
  ta.focus();
  ta.select();
};</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;textarea&gt;foo bar baz&lt;/textarea&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;button onclick="selectBar()"&gt;Select chars 4-7&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick="selectEverything()"&gt;Select everything&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>If you want to style the text with a highlight, you can use a <code>contenteditable</code> div and <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code>:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const reset = () =&gt;
  document
    .querySelector("[contenteditable]")
    .setHTML("foo &lt;mark&gt;bar&lt;/mark&gt; baz");</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>[contenteditable] {
  border: 1px solid black;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div contenteditable="true"&gt;foo &lt;mark&gt;bar&lt;/mark&gt; baz&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;button onclick="reset()"&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Doing this dynamically is possible but you'd probably want to clarify your use case since details change how best to implement this.</p>
